{"step_index":1349,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T08:02:56Z","thinking":"The model's tendency to classify vulnerabilities as CWE-22 stems from an imbalanced dataset, where CWE-22 has significantly more examples than other CWE types. The `security_cot.jsonl` file's limited sample size exacerbates this bias. Building a comprehensive and varied dataset with ample examples of each CWE is the key to improving classification accuracy.\n\n\nFocus is now on constructing a comprehensive dataset to refine vulnerability classification, specifically targeting insecure deserialization (CWE-502), cross-site scripting (CWE-79), and server-side request forgery (CWE-918). The dataset will feature multiple examples per CWE across several programming languages, including Python, JavaScript, Java, PHP, and Go. Each example will include vulnerable and secure code patterns alongside detailed root cause analysis and remediation strategies.\n\nThe goal is to provide a balanced and diverse set of examples, covering a broad range of vulnerable patterns and corresponding secure alternatives. This includes exploring insecure deserialization with various library functions and secure alternatives like JSON and Protobuf. XSS examples will encompass reflected, stored, and DOM-based attacks with and without proper sanitization. SSRF vulnerabilities will be analyzed within the context of request handling in Python, Node.js, and Go, illustrating both unsafe and safe implementations.\n\n\nConsidering common vulnerability patterns across different languages is essential for dataset creation. Examples of insecure URL handling in Java and PHP, along with secure URL parsing and allowlists, will be included. Hardcoded credential examples in Python, Node.js, and Java will be added, contrasted with secure practices using environment variables or secret management.\n\nBuffer overflow examples in C, specifically with `strcpy` and similar functions, will be balanced with safe alternatives like `snprintf`. SQL injection examples in Python, Node.js, Java, and PHP using string concatenation will be paired with parameterized query examples. OS command injection examples in Python, Node.js, PHP, and Go, focusing on unsafe functions, will be countered by secure subprocess calls or allowlists.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"c:\\\\Users\\\\k4ran\\\\OneDrive\\\\Desktop\\\\smartAGENT\\\\Fluxnat_Coder_3B_Training.ipynb\"","EndLine":"100","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"Viewing notebook\"","toolSummary":"\"Inspect notebook dataset loading\""}}]}
